CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: it argues a position. Signs, wonders and miracles in Scripture are given by God to a hearer for a purpose the passage states. In the Old Testament they confirm a messenger to Israel, a king of David’s line or the nations that meet Israel. In the Gospels they are done before Israel, and the Lord names the sign of Jonah for the generation that demands one. The Twelve are sent to the lost sheep of the house of Israel with power over sickness, devils and death, and Acts 1 to 8 reports the signs in Jerusalem, the cities round about and Samaria. Acts 9 to 28 reports signs under the hands of Paul and his companions in settings shaped by Israel’s Prophecy Program, and the volume reports them without assigning them to a single program. The last sign the book records is the healing on Malta, in Acts 28:8, 9."),
("p", "Acts 28 places the leaders of Israel under the verdict of Isaiah 6 and sends the salvation of God to the Gentiles. The book closes on two years of preaching and teaching. With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically, and signs do not continue into the Mystery Program. The sentence governs the programs and makes no statement about God’s power. The passages on gifts, tongues and prophecy in Paul’s letters are read in Volumes 2 and 3, and the apostolic offices in Volume 4. Any reading that adds a promise of signs to the passages read here goes beyond what the text states."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("What is a sign?", "An act, object or person given by God to a hearer for a stated purpose; not only a miracle", "Gen 9:12, 13; Exod 31:13; Exod 4:5"),
    ("Who received the Old Testament signs?", "Egypt, Israel, kings of David’s line, Zion and the remnant", "Exod 7:5; Num 14:11; Isa 7:11; Joel 2:32"),
    ("What do the signs of a messenger prove?", "That the LORD has sent him and that the word is the LORD’s", "Exod 4:5; 1 Kgs 18:36, 37"),
    ("What are the Lord’s signs?", "Done before Israel, cited to Israel, demanded again by an evil generation; the sign of Jonah is given", "Acts 2:22; John 20:30, 31; Matt 12:38–40"),
    ("To whom were the Twelve sent?", "The lost sheep of the house of Israel, with power over sickness, devils and death", "Matt 10:5–8; Luke 9:1, 2"),
    ("What does Hebrews 2:3, 4 say?", "The word was confirmed unto the Hebrew readers by them that heard the Lord", "Heb 2:3, 4"),
    ("What do Acts 1 to 8 report?", "Signs by the apostles, Stephen and Philip, in Jerusalem, the cities round about and Samaria", "Acts 2:43; 5:12–16; 8:6"),
    ("What do Acts 9 to 28 report?", "Signs under Paul’s hands, reported as the text reports them and assigned to no single program", "Acts 14:3; 19:11, 12"),
    ("What does Paul say in his letters?", "The Jews require a sign; Christ wrought signs by him; the signs of an apostle were wrought", "1 Cor 1:22; Rom 15:18, 19; 2 Cor 12:12"),
    ("What is the last sign recorded?", "The healing on Malta", "Acts 28:8, 9"),
    ("What follows in Acts 28?", "The verdict of Isaiah 6, the salvation of God sent to the Gentiles, two years of preaching and teaching", "Acts 28:25–31"),
    ("What does the position say?", "With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at Acts 28, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically; signs do not continue into the Mystery Program", "Acts 28"),
  ], [0.25, 0.48, 0.27]),
]

